WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.452
The bakery has started a little radio show.

2
00:00:05.452 --> 00:00:09.941
Every morning at seven, the head baker reads out the day's bread, live.

3
00:00:09.941 --> 00:00:12.913
Switch on at five past, and you missed the rye.

4
00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.885
The bakery also keeps a recording of each show.

5
00:00:15.885 --> 00:00:19.236
Anyone who presses play hears it from the first word.

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.079
So the live show is shared, and goes on whether anyone listens.

7
00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:25.707
The recording starts again for each listener.

8
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:29.402
Reactor's publishers come in the same two kinds.

9
00:00:29.402 --> 00:00:38.512
And here's the question that's easy to be shy about: when two screens subscribe to the same Flux, do they share one stream, or does each get its own?

10
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.505
Let's shrink it.

11
00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:47.237
The oven thermometer sends a reading every 100 milliseconds, five readings, 200 to 204.

12
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:50.488
Screen A, in the shop, subscribes at once.

13
00:00:50.488 --> 00:00:55.208
Screen B, in the kitchen, switches on 250 milliseconds later.

14
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.780
First, the plain feed.

15
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.981
How many connections does the oven see, and what does screen B get?

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.172
Two connections.

17
00:01:05.172 --> 00:01:10.593
And B gets all five readings, 200 to 204, from the start, on its own copy.

18
00:01:10.593 --> 00:01:16.820
It's the recording: each listener who presses play gets the show from the first word.

19
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.158
A publisher like that is called cold.

20
00:01:19.158 --> 00:01:24.115
It does its work again for every subscriber, like the birthday that needed two cakes.

21
00:01:24.115 --> 00:01:29.713
And with six screens on one thermometer, that's six connections to the oven, where one would do.

22
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:32.520
A hot publisher is the live show.

23
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:39.118
It produces whether or not anyone listens, and each subscriber hears what's sent from the moment it joins.

24
00:01:39.118 --> 00:01:47.084
Reactor makes a cold feed hot with a few operators, and they differ in two things: when they connect, and what a latecomer gets.

25
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.943
The simplest is share: one connection, shared by everyone.

26
00:01:51.943 --> 00:01:54.524
Screen B joins at 250 milliseconds.

27
00:01:54.524 --> 00:01:56.220
What does it get first?

28
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:01.599
202, the reading sent at 300.

29
00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:09.882
Readings 200 and 201 went out before B switched on, and nobody rewinds a live show for a late listener.

30
00:02:09.882 --> 00:02:12.215
The oven sees one connection.

31
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.558
For a screen, that's a blank face until the next reading.

32
00:02:15.558 --> 00:02:19.880
With a thermometer that reads once a minute, that's up to a minute of nothing.

33
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:25.381
So let the show repeat its last announcement for anyone who tunes in.

34
00:02:25.381 --> 00:02:27.376
That's replay(1).refCount().

35
00:02:27.376 --> 00:02:31.008
B gets 201 the moment it joins, then the live ones.

36
00:02:31.008 --> 00:02:32.504
Still one connection.

37
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:38.778
And cache is a recorder that keeps the whole broadcast, and plays it to every newcomer from the start.

38
00:02:38.778 --> 00:02:41.354
B gets all five, the two it missed at once.

39
00:02:41.354 --> 00:02:45.009
On a feed that never ends, cache keeps every reading forever.

40
00:02:45.009 --> 00:02:49.803
That's our doorstep again, only this time we're piling up the loaves on purpose.

41
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.643
The refCount part is the switch on the transmitter.

42
00:02:53.643 --> 00:02:58.834
It connects when the first listener arrives, and switches off when the last one leaves.

43
00:02:58.834 --> 00:03:03.726
Here the radio picture breaks: a real station keeps broadcasting to an empty room.

44
00:03:03.726 --> 00:03:07.843
With refCount, the show stops, and the next listener starts it again.

45
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.510
One guardrail.

46
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:16.335
A shared feed asks the source only for what every subscriber can take, so one slow screen slows them all.

47
00:03:16.335 --> 00:03:19.649
Pair it with a loss policy from the packer's table.

48
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.082
Sometimes the values don't come from a publisher at all, but from ordinary code.

49
00:03:25.082 --> 00:03:30.993
Each till in the shop rings a bell when an order is placed, and every screen in the kitchen hears it.

50
00:03:30.993 --> 00:03:34.329
Someone who walks in later didn't hear the earlier rings.

51
00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:38.104
A sink is Reactor's safe way to ring that bell from code.

52
00:03:38.104 --> 00:03:43.527
A till calls tryEmitNext with the order, and the kitchen screens subscribe to asFlux.

53
00:03:43.527 --> 00:03:49.079
A multicast sink sends each order to whoever is subscribed at that moment, so it's hot.

54
00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:53.700
Now two tills ring at exactly the same moment, on two threads.

55
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.717
What happens to the two orders?

56
00:03:58.733 --> 00:03:59.580
One goes in.

57
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.802
The other call gets back FAIL_NON_SERIALIZED, and its order is never sent.

58
00:04:04.802 --> 00:04:11.153
That's rule 1.3 from the start of this track: a subscriber never gets two signals at once.

59
00:04:11.153 --> 00:04:13.693
So the sink refuses the second ring.

60
00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:19.886
The lesson's demo let 8 threads ring 10,000 times each.

61
00:04:19.886 --> 00:04:23.335
The kitchen heard 11,679 of 80,000.

62
00:04:23.335 --> 00:04:31.020
The sink said no 68,321 times, very politely, with a return value nobody read.

63
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.208
And here the bell picture breaks.

64
00:04:33.208 --> 00:04:36.128
Two hands on a real bell rope still make a ring.

65
00:04:36.128 --> 00:04:38.196
The sink makes one of them let go.

66
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:47.145
So check every result, or ring with emitNext and a busyLooping handler, which tries again until the sink is free, for up to a second.

67
00:04:47.145 --> 00:04:49.397
Then the kitchen heard all 80,000.

68
00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:59.953
Here it is in Java and Kotlin: the thermometer shared with replay(1), with a 5 second grace before it disconnects, and a bell the tills ring safely.

69
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.909
So, do two screens share one Flux?

70
00:05:02.909 --> 00:05:04.445
Only if you make it hot.

71
00:05:04.445 --> 00:05:08.284
Cold plays the recording from the start for each subscriber.

72
00:05:08.284 --> 00:05:12.699
Hot is the live show, and the operator decides what a latecomer gets.

73
00:05:12.699 --> 00:05:15.323
And a sink is a bell your own code rings.

74
00:05:15.323 --> 00:05:17.883
From many threads, ring it with a retry.

75
00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:18.962
Quick check.

76
00:05:18.962 --> 00:05:25.227
With replay(2).refCount(), what does screen B get when it joins at 250 milliseconds?

77
00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:33.107
200 and 201 at once, the last two readings.

78
00:05:33.107 --> 00:05:36.259
Then 202, 203 and 204, live.
